ritain, a further 600 million people use it, alongside other languages, as a second language — in countries like Nigeria, India and the Philippines. And another one billion people are studying it, at any one time, as a foreign<br/>
language, in countries like China, South Korea, France, Germany, Russia and Brazil.</p><p style="text-align:right"><span style=""><em>Jack C. Richards. <strong>Key issues in language learning.</strong> CUP, 2015 (adapted).</em></span></p><p style="text-align: left;">Based on the ideas and linguistic aspects of the text above, judge the item below.</p><p style="text-align: left;">The word “economic”, in “economic inequality”, could be replaced by <strong>economical,</strong> without changing the meaning of the text.</p>', 'judge the item below. The word “economic”, in “economic inequality”, could be replaced by economical, without changing the meaning of the text.', true, '2026-01-30 18:26:23.6685', '2026-01-30 18:26:23.6685', 'ERRADO', '', false);
